<body>
<h1>Unit 21: Potential &amp; Potential Energy</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">Potential is the scalar alternative to the vector field. Because only differences are measurable, the zero of potential is a convention - and because the field is conservative, path never enters the answer.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Electric potential is energy per unit charge, V = U/q, and a potential difference is the work done per unit charge by the field.</li><li>For a point charge V = kq/r with V tending to zero at infinity, and the potential difference is dV = -E.dl along any path.</li><li>Only potential differences are physically meaningful; the choice of zero is a convention, and it changes no experiment.</li><li>The potential energy of a system of point charges is the sum of pairwise terms q1 q2/(4 pi epsilon0 r_ij); the self-energy of a continuous distribution requires a self-consistent calculation.</li><li>Equipotential surfaces are perpendicular to field lines, and the work in moving a charge between two points on the same equipotential is zero.</li><li>Potential is a scalar, which is why circuit and field problems are often shorter in potential language than in field language.</li><li>Potential is defined only up to an additive constant, so only differences are physical; a conductor is one equipotential surface, and the field is minus the gradient of the potential.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The 1/r potential law assumes a point charge in vacuum; for a conductor the entire surface is one equipotential at a single value.</li><li>Potential differences are path-independent, so only the endpoints matter - but a potential difference between two points is not simply E.d unless the path is straight and along the field.</li><li>Moving a charge changes the stored energy of the whole system, and the work is shared between the field and the agent that moves it.</li><li>In a medium with permittivity epsilon the potential of a charge is reduced by the factor epsilon_r; the free charge does not change, the field does.</li><li>The expression U = qV holds for a conservative, time-independent field; in a time-varying field the work done on the charge also changes the energy stored in the field itself.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Potential difference, potential due to a point charge, potential energy, electron volt</li><li>Equipotential lines and surfaces</li><li>Potential gradient</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A charge at a point where the potential is zero must have zero potential energy.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Potential energy of a charge is q V, and the zero of potential is a matter of convention, not of physics. Moving the zero changes every potential energy by the same constant amount.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Only differences in potential are physical. A charge can have a large potential energy in one convention and a small one in another, and the force it feels depends only on the gradient of the potential, never on the value at a point.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A negative potential means a negative potential energy and therefore a force pushing the charge the wrong way.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The sign of the potential energy depends on the sign of the charge as well as on the potential, and the force is set by the field, which points from high to low potential.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For a negative charge, U = q V is positive where V is negative, and the force on it is F = q E, which points towards higher potential. The rule to remember is: a positive charge moves towards lower potential, a negative charge towards higher potential.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Because potential is a scalar, the work done to bring a charge in from infinity is independent of the path taken.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That is right, and the reason is that the electrostatic field is conservative - the work depends only on the endpoints. But the path independence comes from the field, not from the potential being a scalar.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The field of a charge distribution is conservative, so the line integral of E . dl depends only on the end points and equals -(V_final - V_initial). Scalars alone would not guarantee this: the magnetic field is also expressible with a scalar potential in some situations, yet the work done by it around a closed loop is generally not zero.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>Find the potential at a point 30 cm from a point charge of +2.0 microcoulomb in air, and the potential energy of an electron at that point.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> V = k q / r = 8.99 x 10^9 x 2.0 x 10^-6 / 0.30 = 5.99 x 10^4 V (about 60 kV). The potential energy of the electron is U = q V = (-1.602 x 10^-19)(5.99 x 10^4) = -9.6 x 10^-15 J, which is -60 eV, since an electron volt is 1.602 x 10^-19 J. The negative sign reflects attraction to the positive source charge.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the potential at a point a distance r from a point charge Q, and then the potential energy of a second charge q placed there.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The potential difference is defined by V_B - V_A = -integral(A to B) E . dl. For a point charge the field is radial, E = k Q / r^2, so moving from a point at infinity to a point at distance r gives V(r) - V(infinity) = -integral(infinity to r) k Q / r'^2 dr' = + k Q / r. With V(infinity) = 0, V = k Q / r, and more generally V(r) = k Q / r + constant. The potential energy of a charge q at that point is U = q V = k q Q / r, since the definition of the potential energy of a charge in a field is U = q V. Note that only differences in V, and hence only differences in U, are physically meaningful.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A proton is released from rest in the field of a fixed negative charge. Describe the motion in terms of potential and potential energy, and say what quantity is conserved, and why the proton's speed increases even though its potential energy decreases.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The proton starts at some potential V and potential energy U = e V, and the field accelerates it towards the negative charge, i.e. towards higher potential. As it moves, its potential energy decreases and its kinetic energy increases by exactly the same amount, because the field is conservative and the work done by it is the negative of the change in potential energy. The total energy U + K is therefore conserved; no energy is created, it is converted from potential to kinetic form. The proton's speed is greatest near the source charge, where the potential energy is most negative, and would become infinite only if it were allowed to spiral into the source - in practice it radiates and the classical picture breaks down.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Potential of one volt</td><td>1 joule per coulomb</td></tr><tr><td>Field between the plates of a charged parallel-plate capacitor</td><td>sigma/epsilon0</td></tr><tr><td>Potential of the Earth's surface relative to infinity</td><td>about -1.0e5 V</td></tr><tr><td>Energy gained by an electron accelerated through 100 V</td><td>about 1.6e-17 J (100 eV)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How much energy is stored in an electromagnetic field, and how is it distributed in space?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Classically the energy density is (1/2) epsilon E^2 plus a magnetic term; the quantum description in terms of photons remains less intuitive.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can energy be extracted usefully from the vacuum?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Casimir and related effects are real and measurable but tiny; extracting macroscopic energy from vacuum fluctuations is not established.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the potential on the axis of a uniformly charged disc and identify its far-field limit.</li><li>Show in one dimension that E = -dV/dx, using a finite-difference argument rather than calculus.</li><li>Compare the energy stored in a charged capacitor with the work required to charge it, and explain why they are equal.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/potential-potential-difference-and-potential-energy">Read the chapter notes: Potential, Potential Difference and Potential Energy</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-electro">Electrostatics theory lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-symbols-electricity">Electricity symbols reference</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-1-electric-potential-energy">OpenStax: Electric Potential Energy</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-2-electric-potential-and-potential-difference">OpenStax: Electric Potential and Potential Difference</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-5-equipotential-surfaces-and-conductors">OpenStax: Equipotential Surfaces and Conductors</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
